enceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19762342"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"2197274"}],"value":"Catalyzes the deamination of various vicinal amino-alcohols to oxo compounds (PubMed:19762342). Ethanolamine ammonia-lyase (EAL) allows bacteria to utilize ethanolamine as the sole source of nitrogen and carbon in the presence of external vitamin B12. It is spontaneously inactivated by its substrate and reactivated by EutA (PubMed:15466038). Directly targeted to the BMC. May play a role in bacterial microcompartment (BMC) assembly or maintenance (By similarity)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"commentType":"CATALYTIC ACTIVITY","reaction":{"name":"ethanolamine = acetaldehyde + NH4(+)","reactionCrossReferences":[{"database":"Rhea","id":"RHEA:15313"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15343"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:28938"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:57603"}],"ecNumber":"4.3.1.7","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00601"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15466038"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19762342"}]}},{"commentType":"COFACTOR","cofactors":[{"name":"adenosylcob(III)alamin","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00601"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19762342"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"20519496"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"21142024"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"29797764"}],"cofactorCrossReference":{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:18408"}}],"note":{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00601"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"20519496"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"21142024"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"29797764"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"19762342"}],"value":"Binds 1 AdoCbl between the large and small subunits, with 6 cofactors per holoenzyme."}]}},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15466038"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19762342"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"15466038"}],"value":"Inhibited by 5-adeninylpentylcobalamin (AdePeCbl), a cofactor analog (PubMed:19762342). Irreversibly inhibited during catalysis by cleavage of the Co-C bond of the cobalamin coenzyme (Probable) (PubMed:19762342). Reactivated by EutA, which probably involves an ATP-dependent cobalamin exchange (PubMed:15466038)"}],"commentType":"ACTIVITY REGULATION"},{"commentType":"BIOPHYSICOCHEMICAL PROPERTIES","kineticParameters":{"michaelisConstants":[{"constant":8.2,"unit":"uM","substrate":"ethanolamine","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19762342"}]},{"constant":0.055,"unit":"uM","substrate":"adenosylcob(III)alamin","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19762342"}]}]}},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00601"}],"value":"Amine and polyamine degradation; ethanolamine degradation"}],"commentType":"PATHWAY"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19762342"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"20519496"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"21142024"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"29797764"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"20519496"}],"value":"The basic unit is a heterodimer, which further associates into a trimer of heterotetramers (PubMed:19762342, PubMed:20519496, PubMed:21142024, PubMed:29797764). 6 large subunits form a core ring with 6 small subunits projecting outwards (Probable)"}],"commentType":"SUBUNIT"},{"commentType":"SUBCELLULAR LOCATION","subcellularLocations":[{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00601"}],"value":"Bacterial microcompartment","id":"SL-0544"}}]},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15466038"}],"value":"When grown in ethanolamine and adenosylcobalamin (AdoCbl) (at protein level). Note this experiment was done in strain JM109, which expresses the eut operon"}],"commentType":"INDUCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19762342"}],"value":"Partial trypsin digestion of this subunit (removing the first 28 residues) yields an active protein less susceptible to aggregation"}],"commentType":"DOMAIN"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00601"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"Belongs to the EutC family"}],"commentType":"SIMILARITY"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"9278503"}],"value":"In strain MG1655 the eut operon is interrupted by the CPZ-55 prophage, encoding 9 genes situated between eutA and eutB, which are translated in the other direction. CPZ-55 may prevent expression of the eut operon in strain MG1655. Strain W3110 does not have this prophage element and should be able to express the operon"}],"commentType":"CAUTION"}],"features":[{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":295,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Ethanolamine ammonia-lyase small subunit","featureId":"PRO_0000205988"},{"type":"Binding site","location":{"start":{"value":207,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":207,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"","featureCrossReferences":[{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:18408"}],"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00601"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"20519496"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"21142024"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0007744","source":"PDB","id":"3ABO"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0007744","source":"PDB","id":"3ABQ"},{"